{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully. The request is asking me to implement a `solve(case)` function for a grammar-guided node reduction problem.\n\nLet me understand the problem:\n1. We have a parse tree with nodes, each having: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n2. We need to reduce the tree by removing nodes\n3. Rules:\n   - Never remove root, nodes with required symbols, or nodes with retained children\n   - When tentatively removing a node, all required symbols must still be represented\n   - Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (depth-first, deeper first)\n   - Use node ID as stable tiebreak at equal depth\n   - Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order\n\nLet me think about the algorithm:\n\n1. Build the tree structure\n2. Process nodes in order: deeper nodes first, ties broken by node ID\n3. For each candidate node (removable=True):\n   - Check if node's symbol is a required symbol - if so, can't remove\n   - Check if node has any retained children - if so, can't remove\n   - Check if removing this node still leaves all required symbols represented\n   - If all checks pass, remove the node\n4. Return retained node IDs (sorted) and removal order\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo the constraints are:\n1. Node must be removable=True\n2. Node's symbol must not be in required_symbols (or wait - \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is required?)\n3. Node must have no retained children\n4. After removal, all required symbols remain represented\n\nActually, \"required symbols\" - I need to think about this. The required_symbols list contains symbols like \"root\", \"s1\". A node \"has a required symbol\" if its symbol is in required_symbols. So we can never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nWait, but root has symbol \"root\" and required_symbols includes \"root\". And \"removable\": false for root. So that's consistent.\n\nFor n1 with symbol \"s1\" and required_symbols includes \"s1\" - n1 has removable=false, which is consistent.\n\nBut n4 also has symbol \"s1\" and is removable=true. Can we remove n4? If n1 is still present and has symbol \"s1\", then \"s1\" is still represented. So yes, n4 can be removed as long as s1 is still represented elsewhere.\n\nSo the rule \"Never remove a required symbol\" means: don't remove a node if, after removal, the required symbol set would no longer be fully covered.\n\nActually re-reading: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" - I think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols AND its removal would leave that symbol unrepresented.\n\nOR it could mean: never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, period.\n\nBut that seems too strict - n4 has symbol \"s1\" and s1 is required, but if n1 (also s1) remains, then...\n\nActually looking at the next rule: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This suggests we do check if all required symbols remain after removal. So \"never remove a required symbol\" probably means: never remove a node if doing so would make a required symbol unrepresented.\n\nLet me reconsider: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" here means a node whose symbol appears in required_symbols AND is the last/only node with that symbol. But actually, the cleaner interpretation is the next rule covers this: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nSo the algorithm:\n1. Build the tree\n2. Order nodes by (depth descending, id ascending) - deeper first, ID tiebreak\n3. For each node in that order:\n   - Skip if removable=False\n   - Skip if node has retained children\n   - Check if removing this node would leave all required symbols represented in the remaining set\n   - If yes, remove it (add to removal_order)\n4. Return retained nodes (sorted by ID) and removal_order\n\nLet me think about the ordering more carefully. \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\nSo ordering is: sort by depth descending, then by node_id ascending (lexicographic? or as a string?).\n\nLooking at the node IDs: \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\". These are strings. For the tiebreak, we'd sort alphabetically.\n\nLet me trace through case fg1-se-pe-01-a-000:\n\nNodes:\n- root: depth=0, removable=false, symbol=root, utility=1.0\n- n1: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=s1, utility=0.87578184\n- n2: depth=1, removable=true, symbol=s2, utility=0.54663808\n- n3: depth=1, removable=false, symbol=s0, utility=0.43607337\n- n4: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=s1, utility=0.66557814, parent=n2\n- n5: depth=2, removable=false, symbol=s2, utility=0.70145921, parent=n3\n- n6: depth=2, removable=true, symbol=s0, utility=0.19506725, parent=n1\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nOrder by depth desc, then id asc:\n- depth=2: n4, n5, n6 (sorted by id: n4, n5, n6)\n- depth=1: n1, n2, n3\n- depth=0: root\n\nProcessing:\n1. n4: removable=true, no children (leaf), symbol=s1\n   - If we remove n4, remaining symbols: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2), n6(s0)\n   - required_symbols \"root\" and \"s1\" both still present\n   - Remove n4! removal_order = [n4]\n\n2. n5: removable=false → skip\n\n3. n6: removable=true, no children (leaf), symbol=s0\n   - If we remove n6, remaining symbols: root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2)\n   - required_symbols \"root\" and \"s1\" both still present\n   - Remove n6! removal_order = [n4, n6]\n\n4. n1: removable=false → skip\n\n5. n2: removable=true\n   - Children of n2: n4 (but n4 was removed)\n   - So n2 has no retained children\n   - If we remove n2, remaining symbols: root(root), n1(s1), n3(s0), n5(s2)\n   - required_symbols still covered\n   - Remove n2! removal_order = [n4, n6, n2]\n\n6. n3: removable=false → skip\n\n7. root: removable=false → skip\n\nRetained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5\nSorted: [n1, n3, n5, root]\n\nWait, how do we sort retained IDs? The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - sorted lexicographically? Or by some other criterion?\n\nLet me look at node IDs: \"root\", \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\". Lexicographic sort would be: \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\", \"root\" (since 'n' \u003c 'r').\n\nSo retained sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\n\nNow let me implement this:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)\n    \n    nodes_list = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate required fields\n    required_fields = {\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"}\n    for node in nodes_list:\n        missing = required_fields - set(node.keys())\n        if missing:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node missing fields: {missing}\")\n    \n    # Build node dict\n    nodes = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes_list}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in nodes}\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\n        if node[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            parent = node[\"parent\"]\n            if parent not in nodes:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {parent} not found for node {nid}\")\n            children[parent].append(nid)\n    \n    # Validate: exactly one root (parent=None)\n    roots = [nid for nid, n in nodes.items() if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Expected exactly one root, found {len(roots)}\")\n    \n    # Current retained set\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\n    \n    # Order: depth descending, then node_id ascending\n    order = sorted(nodes.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid][\"depth\"], nid))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for nid in order:\n        node = nodes[nid]\n        \n        # Skip if not removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip if has retained children\n        has_retained_children = any(child in retained for child in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_children:\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if removing this node still covers all required symbols\n        # Symbols represented after removal = symbols of retained nodes except this one\n        symbols_after = set()\n        for other_id in retained:\n            if other_id != nid:\n                symbols_after.add(nodes[other_id][\"symbol\"])\n        \n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after):\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": retained_sorted,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about what fields the output should have. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So the output should have at least those two fields.\n\nAlso, I should consider: what exact field names? The problem says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" But it doesn't specify exact names. Looking at the description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I'll use \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\".\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n- The `case` parameter - is it the whole case object (with case_id and payload) or just the payload? Looking at the fixture, the case has `case_id`, `payload` with `nodes` and `required_symbols`. So `case` is the whole case object and we need `case[\"payload\"]`.\n\nActually, the problem says `solve(case)` and the cases in the fixture have structure `{\"case_id\": ..., \"payload\": {...}}`. So I should access `case[\"payload\"]`.\n\nLet me also handle the case where `case` might just be the payload directly (to be safe):\n\nActually, looking at the fixture more carefully - the cases are:\n```json\n{\n  \"case_id\": \"fg1-se-pe-01-a-000\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"nodes\": [...],\n    \"required_symbols\": [...],\n    \"_case_nonce\": \"...\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSo `case[\"payload\"][\"nodes\"]` and `case[\"payload\"][\"required_symbols\"]`.\n\nLet me also think about whether we need to handle the `_case_nonce` field - probably not, just ignore it.\n\nLet me also re-examine the \"never remove a required symbol\" rule more carefully:\n- \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n- root has removable=false, so that's already handled\n- \"a required symbol\" - I think this means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But if we have multiple nodes with the same required symbol, we can remove one as long as the symbol remains represented.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest reading is:\n1. Never remove a node with removable=false (handles root and other non-removable nodes)\n2. Never remove a node with retained children\n3. Only remove a node if all required symbols remain represented\n\nThis is what my implementation does. The \"never remove the root\" and \"never remove a required symbol\" are handled by:\n- root has removable=false\n- required_symbol check ensures required symbols stay covered\n\nLet me verify with the example traces. Actually, I realize I should also think about whether nodes can become removable when their children are removed. In my current implementation, I process nodes in order, and a node might initially have retained children but after we process (and possibly remove) its children in earlier iterations, it might become eligible.\n\nIn my current implementation, I do check `any(child in retained for child in children[nid])` at the time of processing, and `retained` is updated as we go. Since we process deeper nodes first, children are processed (and potentially removed) before parents. So this should work correctly.\n\nLet me trace case a-000 again more carefully:\n\nInitial retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\n\nOrder (depth desc, id asc):\n- n4 (depth=2), n5 (depth=2), n6 (depth=2)\n- n1 (depth=1), n2 (depth=1), n3 (depth=1)\n- root (depth=0)\n\nn4: removable=true, children of n4 = [] (n4 is a leaf), \n  symbols after removing n4: {root(root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n5(s2), n6(s0)} → {root, s1, s2, s0}\n  required_symbols = {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s2, s0} ✓\n  Remove n4. retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}, removal_order = [n4]\n\nn5: removable=false → skip\n\nn6: removable=true, children of n6 = [] (leaf),\n  symbols after: {root, s1, s2, s0} (from root, n1, n2, n3, n5)\n  required ⊆ remaining ✓\n  Remove n6. retained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}, removal_order = [n4, n6]\n\nn1: removable=false → skip\n\nn2: removable=true, children of n2 = [n4] but n4 is not in retained\n  No retained children ✓\n  symbols after removing n2: {root, s1, s0, s2} (from root, n1, n3, n5)\n  required ⊆ remaining ✓\n  Remove n2. retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}, removal_order = [n4, n6, n2]\n\nn3: removable=false → skip\n\nroot: removable=false → skip\n\nFinal: retained_ids = sorted({root, n1, n3, n5}) = [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nremoval_order = [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\nThis looks reasonable. Let me also think about what to include in the output. The problem says \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\"\n\nMaybe I should also include the utility sum or count? But the problem specifically says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I'll keep it simple.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" - \"exact named fields\" suggests there are specific expected field names. Without a clear specification, I'll use descriptive names.\n\nLet me finalize the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract payload\n    if \"payload\" in case:\n        payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    else:\n        payload = case\n    \n    # Validate required top-level fields\n    for field in (\"nodes\", \"required_symbols\"):\n        if field not in payload:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required field: {field}\")\n    \n    nodes_list = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    if not isinstance(nodes_list, list) or len(nodes_list) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"nodes must be a non-empty list\")\n    \n    # Validate each node\n    required_node_fields = {\"id\", \"parent\", \"symbol\", \"depth\", \"removable\", \"utility\"}\n    for node in nodes_list:\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\n        if missing:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node missing fields: {missing}\")\n    \n    # Build node dict (check for duplicate IDs)\n    nodes = {}\n    for n in nodes_list:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid in nodes:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\")\n        nodes[nid] = n\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in nodes}\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\n        parent = node[\"parent\"]\n        if parent is not None:\n            if parent not in nodes:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent '{parent}' not found for node '{nid}'\")\n            children[parent].append(nid)\n    \n    # Validate exactly one root\n    roots = [nid for nid, n in nodes.items() if n[\"parent\"] is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Expected exactly one root node, found {len(roots)}\")\n    \n    # Validate required symbols are present in the tree\n    tree_symbols = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes_list}\n    missing_required = required_symbols - tree_symbols\n    if missing_required:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Required symbols not present in tree: {missing_required}\")\n    \n    # Current retained set\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\n    \n    # Traversal order: depth descending, node_id ascending (lexicographic)\n    order = sorted(nodes.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid][\"depth\"], nid))\n    \n    removal_order = []\n    \n    for nid in order:\n        node = nodes[nid]\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must have no retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Removing this node must leave all required symbols represented\n        symbols_after_removal = {nodes[oid][\"symbol\"] for oid in retained if oid != nid}\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after_removal):\n            continue\n        \n        # Remove the node\n        retained.discard(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_ids\": retained_sorted,\n        \"removal_order\": removal_order\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks good. Let me format it as the required JSON output.\n\nThe output should be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is the Python source as a string.\n\nLet me write the final JSON. I need to be careful about escaping.\n\nThe Python code as a string needs:\n- Newlines as \\n\n- Backslashes as \\\\\n- Quotes as \\\"\n\nLet me write out the final answer. The format requirement is very strict: single JSON object, no markdown, no preamble.\n\nLet me double-check the implementation once more before writing the final JSON.\n\nOne thing I want to verify - the sorting of retained_ids. \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - I'll use standard lexicographic sort. For the example, sorted({\"root\", \"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\"}) gives [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"] since 'n' \u003c 'r' in ASCII.\n\nThe implementation looks correct. Let me write the JSON.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Extract payload\\n    if \\\"payload\\\" in case:\\n        payload = case[\\\"payload\\\"]\\n    else:\\n        payload = case\\n\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    for field in (\\\"nodes\\\", \\\"required_symbols\\\"):\\n        if field not in payload:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n\\n    nodes_list = payload[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(payload[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    if not isinstance(nodes_list, list) or len(nodes_list) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be a non-empty list\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate each node has required fields\\n    required_node_fields = {\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\", \\\"utility\\\"}\\n    for node in nodes_list:\\n        missing = required_node_fields - set(node.keys())\\n        if missing:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing fields: {missing}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build node dict, check for duplicate IDs\\n    nodes = {}\\n    for n in nodes_list:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid in nodes:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate node id: {nid}\\\")\\n        nodes[nid] = n\\n\\n    # Build children map\\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in nodes}\\n    for nid, node in nodes.items():\\n        parent = node[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in nodes:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent '{parent}' not found for node '{nid}'\\\")\\n            children[parent].append(nid)\\n\\n    # Validate exactly one root (parent=None)\\n    roots = [nid for nid, n in nodes.items() if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Expected exactly one root node, found {len(roots)}\\\")\\n\\n    # Validate all required symbols are present in the tree\\n    tree_symbols = {n[\\\"symbol\\\"] for n in nodes_list}\\n    missing_required = required_symbols - tree_symbols\\n    if missing_required:\\n        raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbols not present in tree: {missing_required}\\\")\\n\\n    # Working retained set\\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\\n\\n    # Traversal order: depth descending, then node_id ascending (lexicographic tiebreak)\\n    order = sorted(nodes.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid][\\\"depth\\\"], nid))\\n\\n    removal_order = []\\n\\n    for nid in order:\\n        node = nodes[nid]\\n\\n        # Node must be marked removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Node must have no retained children\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Removing this node must leave all required symbols still represented\\n        symbols_after_removal = {nodes[oid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for oid in retained if oid != nid}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_after_removal):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Commit removal\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_sorted,\\n        \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01Bh8iD6FnnO2mxtTiJN8vhu","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":2191,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":54,"output_tokens":5733,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4880},"service_tier":"standard"}}